    <h2>What We Do</h2>
    <p>BikeLabs covers four things: gear guides, motorcycle comparisons, workshop how-tos, and moto culture. Every article exists to answer a specific question a rider might have — which helmet certification actually means something, whether a CB650R or an MT-07 suits the riding you do, how to tell when your brake pads are genuinely finished.</p>
    <p>We also build <a href="{{ '/tools/' | url }}">free calculators</a> — gearing, tyre sizing, fuel range, fitment — so you can run the numbers against your own bike instead of taking a spec sheet on faith.</p>

    <h2>How We Work</h2>
    <p>We are a research publication, not a test lab. We do not run a workshop or operate a press fleet, and we do not claim hands-on testing we have not done. What we do is read the standards, cross-reference manufacturer documentation against independent certification data and published testing, and translate the result into something a rider can act on.</p>
    <p>This is a deliberate choice about what we are good at. A great deal of what riders need to know is already documented — in certification standards, in engineering specifications, in published laboratory results — but it sits scattered across technical documents written for engineers rather than riders. Pulling that together accurately is genuinely useful work, and it is honest work, which matters more.</p>
    <p>Our full methodology, including what our ratings do and do not represent, is set out in <a href="{{ '/how-we-research/' | url }}">How We Research</a>.</p>

    <h2>Our Perspective</h2>
    <p>BikeLabs is written for a global audience with particular attention to markets that Western motorcycle media covers thinly. The lightweight and mid-capacity machines that dominate roads across Asia, the gear that has to work in sustained heat and monsoon rain rather than a British autumn, the price points that actually apply outside North America and Europe — these get real coverage here rather than a footnote.</p>

    <h2>Our Standards</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Sourced claims.</strong> Specifications, certifications and test results are traceable to the documentation they came from.</li>
      <li><strong>Stated limits.</strong> Where a question cannot be answered from the public record, we say so rather than inventing an answer.</li>
      <li><strong>Independence.</strong> We do not accept payment for coverage or verdicts. Affiliate commissions never influence a recommendation.</li>
      <li><strong>Clarity over volume.</strong> A guide should be the last thing you need to read before deciding, not the longest.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How We Make Money</h2>
    <p>BikeLabs is funded by advertising and affiliate commissions. When you buy a product through one of our links, we may earn a small commission at no extra cost to you. We disclose this on every article containing affiliate links, and it has no bearing on what we recommend — several of our top picks earn us nothing at all.</p>

    <h2>Corrections and Contact</h2>
    <p>Specifications change and mistakes happen. If you spot an error — a figure that does not match the manufacturer's documentation, a certification described incorrectly, a guide that has gone out of date — we want to know. Corrections take priority over everything else in the inbox.</p>
    <p>Reach us through our <a href="{{ '/contact/' | url }}">contact page</a> or directly at <a href="mailto:info@bikelabs.com"><strong>info@bikelabs.com</strong></a>.</p>

